      {
        "heading": "Learning from disputes",
        "body": [
          "Review closed cases monthly and group them by reason code, product, and customer segment. Look for a process gap that the desk can fix, such as a missing delivery scan or an unclear cancellation policy. Send the client one recommendation with the evidence behind it. A report that only counts wins and losses does not change the next month. Assign the recommendation to a named owner.",
          "Track response time separately from outcome. A fast, complete response that loses can still be a good case, while a late response that wins by luck is not a reliable process. Use the response-time data to adjust staffing around notice volume. The desk should know its capacity before the next peak, not after it. Share the trend with the client each month.",
          "Keep a short log of reversals and appeals so the desk can see where the first response fell short. Review the log with the client's finance owner and pick one evidence gap to fix. Update the checklist and retrain the team on the change. A dispute process that never revisits its losses will repeat the same mistake with a different customer."
        ]
      }
